      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\), we need to analyze the numerator \\(x + 6\\) and the denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\).\n\n1. **Analyze the numerator:**\n   The numerator is \\(x + 6\\). Since \\(x + 6\\) is a linear expression, it is always positive for all real values of \\(x\\). Therefore, \\(x + 6 > 0\\) for all \\(x\\).\n\n2. **Analyze the denominator:**\n   The denominator is \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\). We need to determine when this quadratic expression is positive. To do this, we can use the discriminant of the quadratic equation \\(x^2 + 2x + 7 = 0\\):\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = b^2 - 4ac = 2^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 7 = 4 - 28 = -24.\n   \\]\n   Since the discriminant is negative, the quadratic equation \\(x^2 + 2x + 7 = 0\\) has no real roots. This means the quadratic expression \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is always positive for all real values of \\(x\\). To see this, we can complete the square:\n   \\[\n   x^2 + 2x + 7 = (x + 1)^2 + 6.\n   \\]\n   Since \\((x + 1)^2 \\ge 0\\) for all real \\(x\\), it follows that \\((x + 1)^2 + 6 \\ge 6 > 0\\). Therefore, \\(x^2 + 2x + 7 > 0\\) for all \\(x\\).\n\n3. **Combine the results:**\n   Since both the numerator \\(x + 6\\) and the denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) are always positive for all real \\(x\\), the fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is also always positive for all real \\(x\\). Therefore, the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) holds for all real \\(x\\).\n\nThe solution to the inequality is \\((-\\infty, \\infty)\\). Thus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-\\infty, \\infty)}\n\\]",
